issue_context.body:
While it is quite common to write hexadecimal numbers with the prefix `0x`, this format is (seemingly) not supported by `OSSL_PARAM_allocate_from_text`. The selection of the accepted format is done by the application developer, who can use key names prefixed with "hex" to indicate that hex-characters should be accepted as the parameter value. Even such values don't accept the 0x prefix though.

Would you please consider allowing users to specify 0x prefixed integer values too? In other words, I suggest to allow users to use 0x prefixed values for non-hex-prefixed keys. (Or for the hex-prefixed ones.)

The change seems (to me) backwards compatible: hex-prefixes keys will work as before, the non-hex prefix with decimal numbers will work as before. Only the non-hex prefixed keys with 0x prefixed values will be interpreted as hex numbers. Such values are currently misinterpreted.

From the implementation perspective, one can only replace `BN_dec2bn` by `BN_asc2bn`. (I can make a PR if you agree. I just wanted to discuss it here first.)
```
+++ b/crypto/params_from_text.c
@@ -50,7 +50,7 @@ static int prepare_from_text(const OSSL_PARAM *paramdefs, const char *key,
         if (*ishex)
             BN_hex2bn(tmpbn, value);
         else
-            BN_dec2bn(tmpbn, value);
+            BN_asc2bn(tmpbn, value);

         if (*tmpbn == NULL)
             return 0;
```
